#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088886 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nx^2 -2x +2=0.\n$$\n\nThis quadratic is written in standard form with $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=2.$ However, its discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2}-4ac & =(-2)^{2}-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the quadratic equation has no real solutions. However, if we allow complex solutions, we may solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(2)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 2\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $x^2 -2x +2=0$ has two solutions, $x=1+\\textrm{i}$ and $x=1-\\textrm{i}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "275a04ea-da37-527f-9ade-d57aa888110b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2827 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088887 :tutorial/content "We can also solve a quadratic equation with complex solutions using the square root method.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the solutions of the equation $(x-2)^2=-16.$\n\nThe left-hand side is a perfect square. Therefore, we can solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-16} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ x & =2\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, we need to complete the square first before applying the square root method. Let's see how." :tutorial/id #uuid "145efd5f-c374-5133-89cb-39314be19dcd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2829 :tutorial/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots by Completing the Square"}]}
